something very special here on cbs  morning something you will only see on  cbs mornings it is one of the final  interviews with the beloved actor and  comedian bob saggett you remember saget  was found dead in his hotel room last  weekend he was just 65 years old  shocking find behind his wit and persona  on air and in front of the camera he was  also haunted for years by a devastating  personal loss the death of his older  sister gay from a little-known disease  saggett became a champion of fundraising  and research for that disease it's  called scleroderma  just weeks before he died saggett spent  a day with our doctor john lepuc in los  angeles for one of his final final  conversations about the disease and also  how he personally used humor to cope  with his loss and john joins us this  morning you've said this is one of the  hardest pieces you ever wrote i was  riding it through tears i really i  really was and when i sat down with my  close friend bob saget last month i  never imagined it would be the last time  we would be together  a serious topic was on the agenda but as  you'll see  bob never missed an opportunity to  lighten things up  well let's start it off you and i know  each other we're buddies yeah  when did you realize you were funny  i thought it was funny  when people were laughing me i was like  four years old when we sat down in los  angeles last month bob saget showed me  how being funny helps him roll with the  punches  [Music]  and we are getting a lot of airplanes  it's okay  and this guy's having a new muffler put  in but we didn't meet just to have a few  laughs  bob wanted to share a personal story of  loss that had affected him for almost 30  years i had a sister who passed away  from scleroderma it was a three to four  year process and she was gone  and i  couldn't bear it his older sister gay  seen here with her son adam was only 47  years old when she died in 1994 from  scleroderma i can't get the images of  the end of her life out of my head ever  what were some of her symptoms she had a  lot of fatigue  um  her body she said it felt like her skin  was on fire  she went to  regular medical doctors that said that  it could be lupus it could be mental  illness it could be epstein-barr so it  was sort of the lit named everything  except what it was  scleroderma literally means hard skin  it's an autoimmune disease where excess  collagen causes tissue to lose its  elasticity  symptoms include tightening of the skin  finger and toe pain arthritis muscle  weakness and trouble swallowing it can  also damage internal organs especially  the lungs  it can be a very  terrible  multi-system disease but not in every  patient dr fred wigley is a professor of  medicine at johns hopkins university who  has studied scleroderma for 45 years you  see those dilated capillaries dr wigley  is trained to detect the tell-tale signs  of scleroderma the drugs that we have  now clearly work better when we see  those patients at the very early onset  that time when things are just getting  started in the united states an  estimated 300 000 people have  scleroderma 80 percent are women most  commonly ages 30 to 50. for unclear  reasons the disease is often more severe  in people who are native american or  black i could hardly lift my legs they  were heavy as i was going up the steps  my fingers were cold my face started to  swell up  like gay saggett cheryl adams williams a  dentist saw multiple physicians before  receiving the correct diagnosis i  couldn't hold the small dental  instruments to do root canals  and my hands were swollen they were  inflamed i couldn't bend them at all  after she started taking medication to  suppress her immune system her symptoms  completely resolved this is your sister  yeah  she has an angelic look about her i mean  there's something very  sweet but when gay saggett was diagnosed  much less was known about the disease  and how to treat it we were  all in the room  when she let out her last breath  and if it i don't know how to explain it  but it felt like  i mean i'm gonna go all  woo-woo here but it felt like the soul  going past us  literally felt it i felt my hair kind of  move  um  you know and being an actor that's a  very important thing if your hair gets  out of place  so even at this very serious moment  humor comes in humor is the only way my  family survived it is so healthy to  laugh  and i'm out there doing it and i know  it's healing for people we've already  just now raised about six hundred  thousand dollars through laughter and  star power bob saget raised more than 26  million dollars for the scleroderma  research foundation john stamos of  course  george lopez has always been there  dave chappelle john mayer we're just  asking you to give to the scleroderma  research foundation robin williams did  the event seven times he just any time i  asked him he said okay chief whatever  you need  i can't watch what happened to my sister  happen to more people if i can do  anything  and raising awareness is one of the  things that gets you there  and for for me it's  it's an homage to her  and somehow telling her that her life  had a real purpose her spirit clearly is  here it's in you whether she's  physically here or not let me be so bold  as to say i think your  your sister would be really proud of her  kid brother  thanks for saying that i um  i have a lot to live up to i feel like  to really do her justice is to really  make huge strides in the next decade or  two  and to really help these sweet innocent  victims with this disease  my sister should not be dead  and that's  that's one of the things that's kept me  doing this will keep me doing this until  i'm  gone i just i'll do it when i'm gone  oh my goodness john the book the irony  of this piece you know he did this piece  clearly i know you all are friends but  he wanted to bring attention to  scleroderma yeah and so that's why he  did the piece and now in his death we  are all learning more about scleroderma  that we had known before it's so ironic  to me when did you shoot that interview  december 6th just a month ago and that  last line i'll do it when i'm gone which  is good which is what we're doing  and i think when he said that he sort of  realized he had said i'll do this until  i'm gone i'll do this when i'm when i'm  gone  listen no one could have anticipated his  death the thing that strikes me about  bob's second i'm so sorry i never got a  chance to meet him but the outpouring of  love and support for him and talking  about how kind he was how sweet he was  and what a decent human being i've never  seen an outpouring like not from just  comedians either from all different  types of people  who came in contact with him he was a  love he was sweet he was emotionally  intelligent he was a mush and he would  be so  happy  that we're talking about this finally  the scleroderma research foundation  which is so important to him they got a  million and a half dollar matching gift  after he died wow go on our website you  can see how to how to find more  information i also like what he said  laughter is healing yes i honestly  believe that yeah  absolutely i did think it was  interesting he made a joke in that  moment you pointed it out that seems to  be who he was you know he said it and  then he waited and then he couldn't  resist yeah i know this was tough for  you john thank you i know it was thank  you so much i promised him no thank you  did you delivered you did deliver

